course: everyday
lesson: 14
title: Reading faces and voices
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: People show feelings with their faces, voices and bodies, often before they say a word.
explain: A smile that crinkles the eyes usually means happy; a frown and tight lips may mean angry; a quiet voice and eyes looking down may mean sad or worried.
explain: Signals can be mixed up: people cry when they are very happy, and some people hide their feelings, so the kind thing is to ask: «Are you okay?»
explain: If an adult is shouting, very angry, or acting strangely or confused, the safe choice is to keep your distance and tell an adult you trust.
explain: Example: your friend says «I'm fine», but her voice is shaky and she will not look up — her face and voice tell you more than her words.
explain: A boy in Manchester did not notice his friend's clenched jaw and quiet voice and kept teasing him; the friend finally shoved him, he fell, and they did not speak for weeks.
explain: A girl in Toronto saw that her friend looked pale, would not talk and stared at the floor; she told a teacher, and the friend got help for a problem she had been hiding.
fact: Faces show basic feelings such as happiness, sadness, fear, anger, surprise and disgust.
fact: Wide eyes, raised eyebrows and an open mouth often show surprise or fear; a wrinkled nose and a scrunched-up face show disgust.
fact: A red face, clenched fists or a tight jaw and a loud voice are signs of anger.
fact: A real smile usually crinkles the skin around the eyes, not just the mouth.
fact: The tone of a voice — loud, quiet, shaky or flat — can show how someone feels.
fact: Nervousness can make the voice shake and the hands fidget or tremble.
fact: Body language includes how people stand, how they move their hands and where they look.
fact: People sometimes cry when they are very happy, proud or relieved.
fact: Words and body can say different things; then the body is often closer to the truth.
fact: Asking «Are you okay?» is a simple and kind way to check how someone feels.
fact: If someone is upset with you, staying calm and speaking quietly helps them calm down too.
fact: An adult who is very angry, shouting or acting strangely may be unsafe to be near.
fact: When an adult seems unsafe, move away and tell a trusted adult instead of trying to fix it yourself.
fact: Everyone misreads faces sometimes, so it is fair to check by asking.
fact: Missing the signs that someone is angry or upset can turn a small joke into a real fight.
fact: A friend who goes quiet, stops eating or avoids everyone may need help from a trusted adult.
exercise: How can you tell a real smile from a polite one? | A real smile crinkles the eyes too | A real smile shows all the teeth at once | A real smile lasts only a second | A real smile is always very wide
exercise: Your friend says «I'm fine», but looks down and speaks very quietly. What might be true? | She may be sad or worried | She is certainly feeling fine | She is surely angry with you | She is only bored of talking
exercise: Why do people sometimes cry at a wedding or after a big win? | Strong happy feelings can bring tears too | People always feel sad at such events, deep inside | Tears only ever come from pain | Bright lights at events make eyes water
exercise: What is the kindest way to find out if someone is upset? | Ask them gently, «Are you okay?» | Tell them that they look awful | Ignore them so they feel better | Guess, and tell others what you think
exercise: Why is the tone of someone's voice useful to notice? | It can show feelings that words hide | It always shows exactly what they think | It shows how loud the room is | It shows how clever the person is
exercise: A man in a shop is shouting and throwing things. What should you do? | Move away and tell a trusted adult | Go up to him and ask him nicely to stop | Stand close by and watch him | Laugh so that he calms down
exercise: Why is moving away from an adult who acts very strangely better than helping on your own? | It may be unsafe, and adults can help better | Strange adults always mean to hurt children | Children are never allowed to talk to adults | Helping other people is never a good idea
exercise: How can staying calm help when a friend is angry with you? | Calm voices help others calm down | It makes your friend angrier every time | It shows that you do not care at all | It means you have lost the argument
exercise: What does body language include? | How people stand, move and where they look | Only the words that people choose to say aloud | Only the language that people speak | How tall or how short people are
exercise: A face with wide eyes, raised eyebrows and an open mouth most often shows what? | Surprise or fear | Boredom | Sleepiness | Calm, quiet happiness
exercise: Why might someone hide their feelings? | They may feel shy or not want to worry others | Because some people have no feelings inside | Because hiding feelings is always healthy | Because feelings only show when at home
exercise: Your little brother has a red face, clenched fists and a loud voice. What is he probably feeling? | Angry | Sleepy | Calm | Bored
exercise: If you are not sure how someone feels, what is fair to do? | Ask them; anyone can misread a face | Decide for them and tell everyone else | Copy their face exactly to find out | Say nothing and never ask them at all
exercise: Why does a shaky voice often mean someone is nervous? | Worry tenses the muscles used for speaking | A shaky voice always shows that someone is lying to you | Shaky voices come from eating too fast | Nervous people always have a sore throat
test: Noah's friend laughs loudly, but his eyes stay flat and he quickly looks away. What is a good idea? | Check gently that he is really okay | Decide he is having the best day ever | Tell him to stop laughing at once | Laugh louder to cheer him up more
test: Which is true? | Words and face can sometimes say different things | Faces always show exactly what people feel | Only grown-ups show feelings on their faces | Someone who cries must be sad, every single time
test: In the park, an adult starts yelling at people and walking unsteadily. What should a child do? | Keep away and find a trusted adult | Walk over and ask what is wrong | Go close to film it on a phone | Tell the adult loudly to go home now
test: Grandma cries at your school concert and then hugs you tightly. What is most likely? | She is happy and proud | She is upset with you | She did not like the music | She is hurt and needs a doctor
test: A classmate is angry and shouting at you. What helps most? | Speaking calmly and quietly | Shouting back even louder | Pulling a face at them | Stepping up very close to them
test: If you listened only to someone's words and ignored their face and voice, what could happen? | You could miss that they are upset | You would understand them better | You would know their feelings for sure | You would never misread anyone again
test: Which signs show someone may be nervous? | A shaky voice and fidgeting hands | Relaxed shoulders and slow breathing | A big smile that crinkles the eyes | Leaning back and laughing easily
test: Why is it wise to ask rather than guess how someone feels? | Everyone misreads faces sometimes | Faces never show any feelings | Asking always makes people angry | Guessing is against school rules
test: Olivia scrunches up her face, wrinkles her nose and pushes away her plate. What does she feel? | Disgust | Joy | Surprise | Pride
test: she's smiling but her eyes aren't, real smile? | Maybe not; real smiles crinkle the eyes | Yes, any smile is real | Yes, smiles are only in the mouth | No, real smiles never show teeth at all
test: grandad cried at my concert, was he sad? | Maybe not, people cry when proud or happy | Yes, crying always means sad | He must have been hurt | He didn't like the music and wanted to go home
test: friend's gone quiet, not sure what's up | Ask kindly, are you okay? | Ignore her until she talks | Tell everyone she is angry | Guess and walk off
test: man in the park shouting and acting weird, go see? | No, move away and tell a trusted adult | Yes, go and calm him down | Yes, ask him what is wrong | Stand close and watch him so you can tell the police
test: he's gone red and quiet and his fists are tight but i'm only joking | Stop the joke, he is getting upset | Keep going, he will laugh soon | Joke louder so he gets it | Poke him to cheer him up a bit
test: my mate's been super quiet for days and won't eat lunch | Ask if she is okay and tell a trusted adult | Leave her, it is not your business | Tell everyone so they all ask her what is wrong with her | Ignore it, it will go away
test: Noah says if someone's smiling they can't be upset. true? | No, people sometimes smile to hide how they feel | Yes, smiles always show real feelings | Yes, sad people never smile | Yes, unless they are crying too
test: Mason's mum was shouting in the car park and someone says she's always like that and the police know her | Do not spread it; you only saw one moment, and the rumour could be untrue and hurtful | Spread it, because shouting proves the rumour | Spread it, so others avoid Mason | Spread it, and add that she was crying too
test: kid at school says if someone smiles with just their mouth it means they're lying to you, always | That goes too far; a real smile often crinkles the eyes, but people misread faces, so ask if unsure | He is right, a mouth-only smile always means lying | He is right, crinkly eyes always mean anger | He is right, so never trust any smile
test: heard Isla cried at the awards and everyone's saying she was sad she didn't win more | People can cry when happy or proud, so the rumour may be wrong; ask her if she is okay | It must be true, crying always means sadness | It proves she was jealous of the others | It means she was pretending to be upset
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a genuine smile? | It ordinarily causes the skin about the eyes to crinkle, and not merely the mouth | It involves the mouth exclusively, the eyes remaining unaltered | It is invariably accompanied by laughter | It causes the eyebrows to be drawn sharply together
exam: Identify the course of action to be pursued where an adult appears to be unsafe, being very angry or acting strangely. | To move away and inform a trusted adult, rather than attempting to resolve the matter oneself | To approach the adult and attempt to calm him alone | To remain close by and observe the adult in silence | To shout at the adult in order to stop the behaviour
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning disagreement between words and body language. | Where the two diverge, the body is frequently nearer to the truth | Where the two diverge, the words are invariably the truth | Body language conveys no information of any kind | Words and body language never diverge in children
exam: It is given that a friend has become silent, ceased to eat and avoids all company. Determine the appropriate response. | To recognise that the friend may require help and to inform a trusted adult | To leave the friend entirely alone until the condition passes | To laugh in order to raise the friend's spirits | To tell other children so that they may avoid the friend
